ng on here  is just kind of like the  the psychology or the mental state of it  and  the only thing i can think of with that  is like you know you have like these  experiences where  you're maybe inclined to have a little  bit more of like manic and depressive  episodes that are not like  clinical but just like within like a  normal range but noticeable enough where  it's  inconvenient and like  the low carb seems just to be like a  really good option for a lot of people  to kind of normalize that  and i always wonder if that's why  sometimes you see success with kind of  like some of these carnivore anecdotes  about helping with kind of that sort of  thing because they kind of put them  like if you're carnivore you're almost  by default gonna be  kind of pretty strict ketogenic i guess  you could be eating a ton of protein and  maybe  sidestep that to a degree but my guess  is even though it's hard  yeah and those people are going to be  probably still within a  therapeutic ketogenic range i would  imagine in most cases  and i wonder too if like that helps kind  of just it keeps if you think of like an  upper end of your emotions in a lower  end if it just makes  if it tightens that channel a little bit  and that just gives them a little bit  better of an experience from one day to  the next  and yeah so i see a lot of that too just  like just consistent energy levels and  and that sort of thing too  cool okay well zach um  we're nearing the end is there anything  else you want to mention while you're on  here  and also tell people where they can find  you if they want to follow you on  socials  sure and this is totally my fault i kind  of like  distracted us from the original question  but i'd love to share a project i'm  doing uh  this this fall yeah um  you know i said before i've been done  mostly single day ultra marathons so far  i'm going to kind of step away from that  for a bit i actually already have i'm  preparing for it at the moment where i'm  going to run this  project that is called a  transcontinental run  and the route i'm going to take is from  san francisco to new york city so it's  like a 3000  mile track and the way you kind of do it  is  you get an rv and a chase vehicle and a  small crew  and you basically live out of that rv  for however long it takes  the record for it is uh six weeks six  hours  i should say 42 days six hours and 30  minutes  uh which comes out to just over 72 miles  a day by a guy named pete costelnick  um and pete is uh he's the man when it  comes to this like  long haul type stuff he also has done an  event where he went from the  uh keys alaska down to the florida keys  unsupported which means he just pushed a  stroller the whole way  that's like 5 000 miles he averaged over  50 miles a day  so he's got the record for that i've  never done anything remotely close to  this  so like it is enticing to say okay i'm  gonna go after the record  and i think i'm gonna try to but who  knows like  who knows what's gonna happen in like  that time frame as to whether  you know i'm good at that or not or if  it's something my body will be  receptive to or not but my main draw for  that is uh  there's an organization called fight for  the forgotten that a guy named justin  wren  started and he has done a lot of work  with kind of identifying people who  essentially  aren't even at square one in terms of  being able to kind of progress  and he had identified like the pygmy  tribe over in the congo as one of those  people who  you know when the way i like to kind of  describe it in short is if  the only thing you have time to  accomplish during the day is to acquire  clean drinking water  you're you're at square zero because  you're just making sure your basic needs  get met versus  having the extra free time to you know  actually like you know  focus on like development or any type of  progress  so yeah what he did for them was he  started like raising money raising  awareness to build wells so